    "issued_at": "2026-10-31T06:00:00+00:00",
    "product_id": "2af5c0ef-0989-4a55-af54-ad10ff702117",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 310600\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1100 PM PDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n*  Precipitation chances and cooler temperatures continue through\n   the weekend. Heavy rain and thunderstorms may result in flash \n   flooding at times.\n\n*  Cool and dry conditions for early next week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through Thursday\n\nAn unsettled weather pattern continues through the weekend as an \nupper level trough interacts with anomalous moisture associated with \nthe remnants of Tropical Storm Vance. This afternoon, scattered \nshowers and thunderstorms have been very efficient in the moist \nenvironment, dropping a quick 0.50 inches or more as they move over \nand flash flooding has been reported. Instability has also been \nimpressive early this afternoon which has allowed for strong to \nsevere thunderstorms with hail to develop in portions of Clark \nCounty. Scattered showers and thunderstorms will continue in \nsouthern Nevada and Mohave County which could lead to additional \nimpacts such as heavy rain, flash flooding, and hail. By early \ntonight, this round will have waned as showers become more isolated \nin eastern Lincoln, northeast Clark, and northern Mohave County \ntonight through much of Saturday. This lull in activity will end \nSaturday evening though as the trough approaches. Forcing and \nmoisture will increase with the incoming trough, however there \ncontinues to be uncertainty in how much moisture will advent \nnorthward into the area. The difference between the HRRR and \nRRFS/REFS is vast, especially on Sunday. Favored the RRFS/REFS \nsolution as anomalous moisture is expected to be in place for most \nareas, but low confidence in how much will fall. The highest \nconfidence for rainfall that could be moderate to heavy at times is \nin Mohave County where NBM has a 70%-80% probability for over 1 inch \nof rainfall from Saturday night through Sunday night. This is also \nwhere there is some agreement for at least 200% of normal PWATs \n(with higher anomalies possible). Given this signal for heavy rain \nalong with saturated soils from recent rainfall today- issued a \nFlash Flood Watch for Saturday night through Sunday night in Mohave \nCounty outside the Colorado River Valley. Heavy rain and flash \nflooding is possible outside of this area, and if the ECMWF solution \nwith higher PWATs further north and west verifies- later shifts may \nneed to expand this flood watch. Precipitation will be possible \nareawide. , snow levels will remain high (generally above \n9,0009,500 feet). The peaks of the Sierra and Spring Mountains \ncould pick up several inches of snow, but recreational areas like \nLee Canyon should see little to no accumulation. \n\nAs the trough exits early next week, a tightening surface gradient \nalong its western flank will drive breezy to gusty winds Monday, \nparticularly across the southern Great Basin. Most gusts will \nsubside Monday evening, though terrain-channeled winds through the \nColorado River Valley could linger into Tuesday. Cooler air will \nsettle in behind the system despite shortwave ridging building \noverhead, keeping highs roughly 5 degrees below seasonal normals \nthrough Tuesday under dry conditions. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 06Z Forecast Package...\n\nMost of the shower activity has ended for the overnight period; \nhowever, a stray shower or two can't be ruled out, but confidence is \ntoo low to include in the TAF. CIGs will generally remain above \n5kft, but some lower CIGs down to 2kft will be possible through 08Z. \nWinds will generally be light and variable. Southeast winds will \nincrease after 21Z with gusts up to 18kts. Rain chances will \nincrease late Saturday evening with lowering CIGs and low chances of \nvisibility restrictions. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Most of the shower \nactivity has ended, but some lower CIGs will remain across the Las \nVegas Valley through at least 08z. Elsewhere, generally light winds \nand CIGs 10kft or higher. Rain chances will increase late afternoon \nareawide with lowering CIGs and slight chances of visibility \nrestrictions. The greatest threat of rain and low CIGs will be \nduring the overnight period. \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Nickerson\nAVIATION...Gorelow\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
